PRIVACY
=======

This terminal records the commands typed into it. Here is exactly what that
means, in the detail I would want if I were on your side of the connection.


WHAT IS RECORDED

    the first word of the line      `cd projects` is recorded as `cd`
    whether it is a real command    which is what splits the two lists
    the country                     from Cloudflare's CF-IPCountry header
    the time it arrived             stamped by the server, not by your clock

That is the whole record. One line of the file looks like this:

    {"at":"2026-08-27T19:47:40Z","cmd":"ls","ok":true,"cc":"BR"}


WHAT IS NOT RECORDED

    No IP address.
    No cookie. This site sets none, for anything.
    No session identifier -- not even a random one, which is an identifier
        with a friendlier name.
    Nothing after the first word. Whatever you `echo` stays with you.
    Not your clock. A machine's clock drifts in its own particular way, and
        that drift recognises the machine.

Two lines from the same visitor are indistinguishable from two lines from two
visitors. That is on purpose: it is what stops anyone -- me included -- from
reassembling a session out of the file.


WHAT IT IS FOR

The `stats` command. The commands people run, and the things people typed that
are not commands here, which is the most honest list of what this place is
missing.


WHERE IT GOES

A JSON Lines file on the Raspberry Pi that is serving you this page. It goes
nowhere else. There is no analytics script on this site, no tag manager, and no
third party in the path except Cloudflare, which routes the connection and
tells the server which country it came from.

To keep the write endpoint from being hammered, nginx counts requests per
address in memory. That counter is never written to disk and is gone when the
process restarts.


WHAT STAYS IN YOUR BROWSER

Your command history and your preferences -- font size, CRT -- live in this
browser's localStorage and are never sent anywhere. Clearing this site's data
in your browser removes them. `cat ~/.bash_history` shows you the history part.


THE CODE

    api/usage.py             what writes the file
    src/system/telemetry.ts  what sends it from here

Both are public. If this file and that code ever disagree, the code is what is
actually running, and this file is a bug -- tell me about it.
